<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Filippo Maria Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1392">1392</a>–<a href="/years/1447">1447</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Filippo Maria Visconti (1392–1447) was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> from 1412, the last of the Visconti line. He rebuilt the state of his father, Gian Galeazzo, after its collapse and waged long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, relying on condottieri such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, to whom he married his only child, the illegitimate Bianca Maria. After his death without a legitimate heir, Milan briefly became the Golden Ambrosian Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti.jpg" alt="Filippo Maria Visconti" width="320" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pisanello · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pisanello_-_Codex_Vallardi_2484.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Filippo Maria Visconti was born on 3 September 1392, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> and Caterina Visconti. His father died of plague in 1402, and his elder brother Gian Maria became Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> with their mother's support. Gian Maria came to regard her as a threat and had her imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a> on 18 August 1404; she died there on 17 October, and her death was rumoured to have been caused by poison. After Gian Maria was assassinated on 16 May 1412, Filippo Maria allied himself with Beatrice, the widow of the condottiero Facino Cane, gaining access to her late husband's wealth and armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Filippo Maria succeeded his brother as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and established his rule over Pavia. His succession was contested by his uncle Estorre Visconti and his cousin Giacomo, who briefly held Milan, but on 16 June 1412 he retook the city. In 1418 he had Beatrice executed at the Castle of Binasco after accusing her of adultery. By employing condottieri such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-bussone-da-carmagnola">Francesco Bussone da Carmagnola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-piccinino">Niccolò Piccinino</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, he recovered much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> territory of his father's duchy. His attempt to expand into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> in 1423 provoked a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> joined in 1425. Venice conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, and Filippo Maria was defeated at Maclodio on 12 October 1427. Peace was later signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>.</p><p>In 1428 he married Marie of Savoy, daughter of his ally Amadeus VIII of Savoy, who later reigned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Antipope Felix V</a> with Visconti's support. Filippo Maria also invited the humanist Gasparino Barzizza to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, where he founded a school. The oldest surviving tarot decks, then called carte da trionfi, were likely commissioned by him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Filippo Maria died on 13 August 1447, the last male of the Visconti line. After a brief republican experiment known as the Ambrosian republic, the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, who had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bianca-maria-visconti">Bianca Maria Visconti</a>, Filippo Maria's only child, born to his mistress Agnese del Maino.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Filippo Maria Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Filippo Maria Visconti</b> (r. 1412–1447) captains such as Francesco Bussone da Carmagnola and Niccolò Piccinino reconquered much of Lombardy, but Carmagnola later served Venice and defeated Milan at the Battle of Maclodio in 1427; Venice gained Brescia and Bergamo, and its frontier stayed on the Adda River…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Duke <b>Filippo Maria Visconti</b> died without a male heir in August 1447, a group of Milanese nobles and lawyers proclaimed a republic named after the city's patron saint Ambrose.</blockquote><a href="/state/golden-ambrosian-republic">Golden Ambrosian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1427" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Filippo Maria Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=1427">What was here in 1427? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
